# Cloudflare Pages Redirect Rules
# This file ensures older WordPress URL structures map to the new Next.js routes
# Place this in the 'public' directory of the Next.js app
#
# NOTE: /sitemap.xml, /news-sitemap.xml, and /:slug (social-media OG-tag
# previews) used to be handled here as 200-status "proxy" rules pointing at
# the Worker. Cloudflare Pages' _redirects only supports proxying to
# relative, same-site paths — not external domains — so those rules were
# silently no-ops (Search Console flagged /news-sitemap.xml as returning
# HTML because of exactly this). They're now handled by
# functions/sitemap.xml.ts, functions/news-sitemap.xml.ts, and
# functions/[slug].ts instead, which run as real server-side fetches with
# no such restriction. llms.txt is now a plain static file
# (public/llms.txt) for the same reason — no proxying needed at all.

# General structure redirects
/category/* /categories/:splat 301
/tag/* /tags/:splat 301
/author/* /authors/:splat 301

# WordPress's default permalink structure is /YYYY/MM/DD/post-slug/ — this
# general rule handles ALL such old links at once by keeping only the slug,
# rather than the single hardcoded example URL previously here (which only
# fixed that one specific link; every other WordPress-era dated URL fell
# through to the SPA fallback with no matching article, landing on generic
# homepage content — this is very likely most of the "old WordPress links
# redirect to home" reports, since dated permalinks were WordPress's default).
/:year/:month/:day/:slug /:slug 301
/about-us /about 301

# Fallback for dynamic trailing slash differences
# (Next.js config handles most, but Cloudflare enforces edge rules)
^/(.*)/$ /$1 301

# SPA fallback — any route not matched above or by a real static file /
# Pages Function (e.g. a client-side-only route like /about, /disclaimer)
# serves index.html so App.tsx's own client-side routing can take over,
# instead of Cloudflare Pages returning a blank 404 for it. Must stay last.
/* /index.html 200
